{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["And the reason of <b>\"and Pharaoh awoke, etc.\"</b> — behold, it is a complete dream; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 41:7). In my opinion, it hints that he stood upon his bed, awakening, pondering his dream — perhaps he might yet see something a third time — and when he stood in the morning and dreamt no more, his spirit was troubled. This is the meaning of \"and it came to pass in the morning, and his spirit was troubled.\" But concerning Nebuchadnezzar it says \"and his spirit was troubled, and his sleep was upon him\" (Daniel 2:1), for his heart did not lie down even at night. And Scripture mentioned \"and Pharaoh awoke\" because of the matter discussed in the Book of Sleep: a dream that one dreams after another dream of a different matter in one's sleep is not fulfilled. Therefore it says that when he awoke, the dream was established, and he pondered it until morning — perhaps his dream would be repeated a third time when he fell asleep again. Now Pharaoh himself recognized that it is one matter, and therefore Scripture mentions \"and behold, a dream,\" and thus he said \"I have dreamed a dream, and there is no interpreter for it\" — he did not say \"dreams\" — and this is the meaning of \"and I saw in my dream.\" But the verse mentions \"and there was no interpreter of them for Pharaoh,\" to say that there was no interpreter for even one of them."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:41.808682192Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}